{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"formatting-output","title":"Formatting Output","group":"Getting Started","order":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Two tools: stream <em>manipulators</em> from <code>&lt;iomanip&gt;</code> (classic, sticky state) and C++20's <code>std::format</code> (Python-style format strings, type-safe).</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-braces\"></i><code>std::format</code> (C++20)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;format&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;numbers&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::format(\"{} + {} = {}\\n\", 2, 3, 2 + 3);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::format(\"[{:&gt;6}] [{:&lt;6}] [{:^6}]\\n\", \"r\", \"l\", \"c\");  // align in width 6\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::format(\"{:.3f} {:e}\\n\", std::numbers::pi, 12345.678);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::format(\"{:05d} {:+d} {:#x} {:#b}\\n\", 42, 7, 255, 5);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::format(\"{1} {0} {1}\\n\", \"a\", \"b\");                  // positional\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::format(\"{:*^11}\\n\", \" hi \");                          // custom fill\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 + 3 = 5\n[     r] [l     ] [  c   ]\n3.142 1.234568e+04\n00042 +7 0xff 0b101\nb a b\n*** hi ****</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example → result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>{:&gt;8}</code> <code>{:&lt;8}</code> <code>{:^8}</code></td><td>Right / left / centre in width 8</td><td><code>{:&gt;4}</code>, <code>7</code> → <code> 7</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{:*^8}</code></td><td>Centre with fill character <code>*</code></td><td><code>{:*^5}</code>, <code>\"a\"</code> → <code>**a**</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{:.2f}</code></td><td>Fixed, 2 decimals</td><td><code>3.14159</code> → <code>3.14</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{:e}</code> / <code>{:g}</code></td><td>Scientific / shortest</td><td><code>1500.0</code> → <code>1.500000e+03</code> / <code>1500</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{:05d}</code></td><td>Zero-pad to width 5</td><td><code>42</code> → <code>00042</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{:x}</code> <code>{:#x}</code> <code>{:b}</code> <code>{:o}</code></td><td>Hex, hex with <code>0x</code>, binary, octal</td><td><code>255</code> → <code>ff</code>, <code>0xff</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{:+}</code></td><td>Always show sign</td><td><code>7</code> → <code>+7</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">C++23</div><p>C++23 adds <code>std::print(\"{}\\n\", x)</code> / <code>std::println</code>, which format <em>and</em> write in one call. Until then, <code>std::cout &lt;&lt; std::format(...)</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments-horizontal\"></i>Stream manipulators (<code>&lt;iomanip&gt;</code>)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iomanip&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    double price = 3.14159;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::fixed &lt;&lt; std::setprecision(2) &lt;&lt; price &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; price * 2 &lt;&lt; '\\n';                 // precision is *sticky*\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::setw(6) &lt;&lt; 42 &lt;&lt; '|' &lt;&lt; 42 &lt;&lt; '|' &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // setw applies to next item only\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::setfill('0') &lt;&lt; std::setw(4) &lt;&lt; 7 &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::left &lt;&lt; std::setfill('.') &lt;&lt; std::setw(8) &lt;&lt; \"name\" &lt;&lt; \"Ada\\n\";\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::hex &lt;&lt; 255 &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::dec &lt;&lt; 255 &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::boolalpha &lt;&lt; true &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::noboolalpha &lt;&lt; true &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3.14\n6.28\n    42|42|\n0007\nname....Ada\nff 255\ntrue 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Manipulator</th><th>Effect</th><th>Sticky?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>std::setw(n)</code></td><td>Minimum width of the next field</td><td>No — next output only</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::setprecision(n)</code></td><td>Significant digits (or decimals with <code>fixed</code>)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::fixed</code> / <code>std::scientific</code></td><td>Floating-point notation</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::setfill(c)</code></td><td>Padding character</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::left</code> / <code>std::right</code></td><td>Alignment inside the width</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::hex</code> / <code>std::oct</code> / <code>std::dec</code></td><td>Integer base</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::boolalpha</code></td><td>Print <code>true</code>/<code>false</code> instead of <code>1</code>/<code>0</code></td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Manipulators (except <code>setw</code>) change the stream's state <em>permanently</em>. A <code>std::hex</code> left on <code>std::cout</code> makes every later integer print in hex — reset with <code>std::dec</code>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Prefer <code>std::format</code> in new code — the format is visible in one string and nothing leaks into later output.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Mixing <code>printf</code> and <code>std::cout</code> on the same output without care — their buffers are synchronised only while <code>sync_with_stdio(true)</code> (the default).</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does std::setprecision(3) do without std::fixed?</summary><div><p>It sets the number of <em>significant digits</em>: <code>std::cout &lt;&lt; std::setprecision(3) &lt;&lt; 3.14159</code> prints <code>3.14</code>, and <code>1234.5</code> prints <code>1.23e+03</code>. With <code>std::fixed</code> it means digits <em>after the decimal point</em>.</p></div></details></section>"}